12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 2036);
18. Regional Regulation of Batu Bara Regency Number 1 of 2009 concerning Government Affairs that are the Authority of the Batu Bara Regency Government;

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BATU BARA REGENCY and

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence is implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of Victims;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims;
e. legal certainty.

Article 3

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence aims to:

a. prevent all forms of violence against women and children;
b. protect women and provide services to women and children victims of violence;
c. empower women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right:

a. to have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. to receive protection from family, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a protection order from the court;
c. to recovery of physical, psychological and sexual health according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
d. to special handling related to the confidentiality of victims;
e. to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to spiritual guidance services;
g. to determine their own decisions.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 5

1. The Regional Government is obligated and responsible for providing protection to women and children victims of violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:

a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children victims of violence based on laws and regulations;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
c. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence;
d. allocating budget in the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence;
e. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children victims of violence.

Article 6

1. The community is obligated and responsible for providing protection to women and children victims of violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in the form of:

a. preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;
b. providing protection to victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. providing information and/or reporting acts of violence against women and children to the competent authorities;
e. participating in the handling of victims of violence.

BAB V

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

:Article 7 (1) In providing protection for women and children victims of violence in the Region, the Regent may establish PPT.

2. The establishment and development of PPT shall be adjusted to the development of priority needs, and the financial capacity of the Region as well as the institutional and personnel capacity available in the Region.
3. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as P2TP2A, Safe Houses, Child Social Protection Houses, Trauma Center Protection Houses, Women's Social Protection Houses, Shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
4. Forms of services implemented by PPT include:a. complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal aid;
e. repatriation; and !f. social reintegration.
5. In providing protection for women and children victims of violence PPT is obligated to:a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to Victims;
b. organize Protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to Health Rehabilitation, Social Rehabilitation, Repatriation, social reintegration and legal aid;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants required for Victims;

di' network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of Victims and cooperate with Witness and Victim Protection institutions, social Protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community, or other institutions for victims' health recovery;

:

e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for victims;
f. maintain confidentiality of victims;
g. provide fulfillment of legal aid for victims; h. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out through networking, PPT remains responsible for the entire referral process of services required by victims.
6. Provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be implemented in accordance with SPM.

Article 10

SPM as referred to in Article 9 has performance indicators including:

a. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for women and children victims of violence within the integrated service unit;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for women and children —* victims of violence within the integrated service unit;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children;
f. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal aid services;
g. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; danh. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.
